From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:35:26 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] mesa/st: skip a draw whose uploads the driver refused

A state update that uploads to a buffer the driver allocates for it
assumed the allocation succeeds. The constants update and the update
of zero-stride vertex attributes used the pointer u_upload_alloc()
returns without looking at it, as does pipe_upload_constant_buffer0().
A driver with a bounded memory budget refuses the uploader's next
buffer once its budget is full, the pointer is NULL, and the copy that
follows faults the host.

st_validate_state() now returns false, after raising GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY,
when an update could not get its upload memory. The update marks its
own state dirty again, so the next draw runs it once more, and does
not touch the pipe. Every caller that draws or dispatches skips the
work: st_prepare_draw() returns whether the draw may go on, and so do
the compute dispatches, glDrawPixels, glCopyPixels, glBitmap and
glDrawTex. The vertex-state playback of a display list gives back the
reference it took before the skipped draw.

The zero-stride attribute update still lays out its vertex elements
and leaves the slot without a buffer, which a draw never sees.

pipe_upload_constant_buffer0() returns false when it bound nothing.
The pixel-buffer draw and download, hardware GL_SELECT and the clear
quad fall back or skip: glClear raises GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY as it already
does when the quad's vertices are refused. The HUD, which no host
context shows, ignores the result.

Other callers of u_upload_alloc() and u_upload_data() on the host's
paths already handle a refusal: the quad and texture-draw helpers,
the blitter, u_vbuf, u_primconvert, util_upload_index_buffer() and
the threaded context's uploads.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
